1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,488
The simplest way to run a permanent-magnet
motor:

2
00:00:03,528 --> 00:00:08,609
connect two of its three leads across the
supply, and leave the third one open.

3
00:00:08,649 --> 00:00:10,469
Every 60 electrical degrees,

4
00:00:10,509 --> 00:00:14,455
move on to the next pair, timed by three
Hall sensors.

5
00:00:14,495 --> 00:00:17,734
That's six-step, the classic brushless DC
drive. Today:

6
00:00:17,774 --> 00:00:22,269
why it works, what it costs in torque
ripple, and how to scope one.

7
00:00:23,369 --> 00:00:25,906
First, the answers from Lesson 15.

8
00:00:25,946 --> 00:00:32,235
One: a viscous run-down halves the speed
in 2 s,

9
00:00:32,275 --> 00:00:37,909
so B = J ln 2 / 2 s = 1.04 × 10⁻⁵ N·m·s.

10
00:00:37,949 --> 00:00:46,072
Two: 14.1 V / (√3 × 1257 rad/s) = 6.48
mWb, so λm = 6.5 mWb.

11
00:00:46,112 --> 00:00:51,117
Three: three Hall sensors give six sectors
of 60° per electrical cycle,

12
00:00:51,157 --> 00:00:54,499
one per commutation step: today's drive.

13
00:00:55,599 --> 00:01:01,418
For our BLDC motor, take the variant from
Lesson 7: 180° magnets and

14
00:01:01,458 --> 00:01:08,438
full-pitch coils in a smooth bore. Its
back-EMF has flat tops about 130° wide.

15
00:01:08,478 --> 00:01:12,349
It gets the reference motor's R, L and
inertia,

16
00:01:12,389 --> 00:01:17,741
and the same 6.5 mWb fundamental, so only
the shape differs.

17
00:01:17,781 --> 00:01:22,719
Now drive rectangular currents: +I into
the phase on its positive flat,

18
00:01:22,759 --> 00:01:27,186
−I out of the one on its negative flat,
none in the third.

19
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:33,499
The power is e_a i_a + e_b i_b + e_c i_c.
With two phases conducting,

20
00:01:33,539 --> 00:01:41,675
that's EI twice: at 3000 rpm and 10 A, 2 ×
6.54 V × 10 A = 131 W, a steady 0.416 N·m.

21
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:46,994
The inverter does the job of a DC motor's
commutator: that's brushless DC.

22
00:01:48,094 --> 00:01:51,930
To find the rotor, the drive uses Hall
sensors at the bore:

23
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:56,501
each reads 1 while a north pole faces it,
and 0 for a south pole.

24
00:01:56,541 --> 00:01:59,048
Three sit 120 electrical degrees apart,

25
00:01:59,088 --> 00:02:03,006
so their square waves are shifted by a
third of a cycle.

26
00:02:03,046 --> 00:02:09,519
Together they make six codes, each lasting
60°: 100,

27
00:02:09,559 --> 00:02:16,057
110, 010, 011, 001, 101. 000 and 111 never
occur.

28
00:02:16,097 --> 00:02:21,513
Placed like this, each code also says
where the north pole points,

29
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:25,607
within ±30°: 100 means near phase a's
axis.

30
00:02:26,707 --> 00:02:30,464
Which pair should conduct? For the most
torque, push current into the

31
00:02:30,504 --> 00:02:34,672
phase with the most positive EMF, and pull
it out of the most negative.

32
00:02:34,712 --> 00:02:41,800
Evaluating the EMFs at the centre of each
sector gives the table:

33
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:46,915
100 → B+ C−; 110 → B+ A−; and so on
around.

34
00:02:46,955 --> 00:02:51,957
The floating phase is always the one whose
EMF crosses zero mid-sector,

35
00:02:51,997 --> 00:02:55,949
so each commutation comes 30° after a zero
crossing.

36
00:02:55,989 --> 00:03:01,062
The current vector then sits 90° ahead of
the rotor, give or take 30°.

37
00:03:02,162 --> 00:03:06,892
In the bridge, each sector closes two
switches: the upper one of the high phase,

38
00:03:06,932 --> 00:03:12,061
and the lower one of the low phase. The
third leg is open, so that phase floats.

39
00:03:12,101 --> 00:03:17,535
Each phase conducts 120° positive, floats
60°, conducts 120° negative,

40
00:03:17,575 --> 00:03:22,845
floats 60° again; each switch is on for a
third of the cycle.

41
00:03:23,945 --> 00:03:28,632
Here's the circuit, simulated at 3000 rpm,
with a current loop

42
00:03:28,672 --> 00:03:33,455
chopping the conducting pair at about 25
kHz to hold 10 A.

43
00:03:33,495 --> 00:03:39,307
The blocks can't switch instantly: the
outgoing current decays through a

44
00:03:39,347 --> 00:03:45,640
diode while the incoming one builds,
taking 39 µs with 0.05 mH per phase.

45
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:50,189
The floating terminal carries no current,
so it shows the back-EMF:

46
00:03:50,229 --> 00:03:55,582
half the bus, plus 3/2 of its EMF less the
part common to all three phases.

47
00:03:55,622 --> 00:04:01,058
It crosses 12 V exactly at the zero
crossing, 30° before the next commutation.

48
00:04:01,098 --> 00:04:06,302
Sensorless six-step drives time their
switching from that crossing.

49
00:04:07,402 --> 00:04:13,678
With ideal blocks, the rounded corners of
this EMF leave only 1.3% torque ripple.

50
00:04:13,718 --> 00:04:17,869
The simulation adds chopping ripple and a
short notch

51
00:04:17,909 --> 00:04:22,144
at each commutation; the mean stays at
0.412 N·m.

52
00:04:22,184 --> 00:04:26,944
Near top speed, there's no voltage to
spare. During the overlap,

53
00:04:26,984 --> 00:04:30,619
the third phase's current changes at (V −
4E)/3L:

54
00:04:30,659 --> 00:04:34,669
negative once the EMF passes a quarter of
the bus.

55
00:04:34,709 --> 00:04:40,261
At full duty and 5000 rpm, it loses 44% at
every commutation.

56
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:44,927
Carlson and colleagues analysed this in
1992.

57
00:04:46,027 --> 00:04:50,454
Now six-step our reference motor, whose
EMF is nearly sinusoidal.

58
00:04:50,494 --> 00:04:54,029
Its line-to-line EMF is an arch, not a
plateau,

59
00:04:54,069 --> 00:05:00,262
so even ideal blocks give 26% torque
ripple. For a pure sine, it would be 14%.

60
00:05:00,302 --> 00:05:03,501
That's Lesson 13's Hall-sensor angle
error,

61
00:05:03,541 --> 00:05:08,486
up to 30° either way, swept six times per
electrical cycle.

62
00:05:09,586 --> 00:05:12,789
Without a current loop, the duty sets the
speed.

63
00:05:12,829 --> 00:05:16,775
Chop the upper switch at 20 kHz, with the
lower one of that

64
00:05:16,815 --> 00:05:21,128
leg filling the gaps, and the pair sees d
× 24 V on average.

65
00:05:21,168 --> 00:05:28,062
Unloaded, nearly all of it balances the
pair's back-EMF, so speed is proportional

66
00:05:28,102 --> 00:05:35,211
to duty: 5530 rpm at full duty, or 231 rpm
per volt, the Kv on hobby datasheets.

67
00:05:35,251 --> 00:05:41,232
Simulated start-ups settle on that line.
At full duty, 10 A of load costs

68
00:05:41,272 --> 00:05:47,464
about 470 rpm: 185 for the resistance, the
rest for the commutations.

69
00:05:48,564 --> 00:05:53,229
So, six-step or sinusoidal drive? Six-step
needs three Hall sensors,

70
00:05:53,269 --> 00:05:57,335
one current sensor in the DC link, and a
six-row table.

71
00:05:57,375 --> 00:06:01,400
Sinusoidal drive needs a continuous angle,
two phase currents,

72
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:06,289
Clarke, Park and PI loops, but gives a
sinusoidal motor smooth torque.

73
00:06:06,329 --> 00:06:10,732
Our simulations assume ideal switches and
Hall sensors, no dead time,

74
00:06:10,772 --> 00:06:14,943
constant speed, linear inductance, and no
cogging torque.

75
00:06:16,043 --> 00:06:19,407
On the bench: a Hall-sensored motor,
clamped down,

76
00:06:19,447 --> 00:06:23,723
its six-step controller, and a current-
limited 24 V supply.

77
00:06:23,763 --> 00:06:29,622
Scope the three Hall outputs against the
controller's ground. Expect the

78
00:06:29,662 --> 00:06:35,902
six codes in order, or in reverse, with an
edge every 833 µs at 3000 rpm.

79
00:06:35,942 --> 00:06:41,399
Clamp current probes on two phase leads:
120° blocks with chopping ripple,

80
00:06:41,439 --> 00:06:45,785
a 60° gap, and a short transition at every
commutation.

81
00:06:45,825 --> 00:06:49,872
Safety: no propellers, fingers clear of
the rotor, and never clip a

82
00:06:49,912 --> 00:06:54,865
scope ground to a phase lead; use
differential probes for phase voltages.

83
00:06:55,965 --> 00:06:58,245
Three predictions before next time.

84
00:06:58,285 --> 00:07:02,863
One: how many conduction states does six-
step use per electrical cycle?

85
00:07:03,863 --> 00:07:05,370
Two: on our 8-pole motor,

86
00:07:05,410 --> 00:07:09,561
how many mechanical degrees are there
between commutations?

87
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:15,404
Three: on a 24 V bus, what line-to-line
peak does six-step apply,

88
00:07:15,444 --> 00:07:18,724
and what can sine-triangle PWM reach?

89
00:07:19,724 --> 00:07:26,855
Next time: the three-phase inverter, and
how PWM turns 24 V pulses into sine waves.
